--- Windows target (Wine) ---
Running `cargo test --target x86_64-pc-windows-gnu` using Wine wrapper.
Wine configuration failed due to missing X server / `wine32` architecture limits in the container environment.
Because Wine failed to initialize properly, no test results could be gathered natively for the Windows target under Wine. Note that Wine is NOT proof of real Windows behaviour.

Code inspection for Windows problems:
- IPC paths use Named Pipes which do not suffer from max length 260 limit naturally when correctly prefixed (our path is `\\.\pipe\rat` and in tests `\\.\pipe\rat_test_{pid}`, well under limits).
- Test edge cases run natively on linux tests, verifying non-ASCII Unicode (Persian, Chinese) paths and backslashes in simulated payloads. Serde serializes and deserializes these correctly since everything uses UTF-8 strings by default.
- No direct file locking is used in `rat-daemon` itself outside of standard socket management and pipe handle closures (handled by `tokio`), except for Linux's socket file deletion. Windows pipe lifecycle is bound to the server instance correctly.
- CRLF line endings on IPC inputs? `LinesCodec` by default splits by `\n` but strips trailing `\r` (see `LinesCodec` implementation in tokio-util which handles both `\n` and `\r\n`).
--- Performance and Budget ---
Added criterion benchmark testing serialization of 2,000 sockets.
Added `test_serialization_budget` verifying that processing 2,000 sockets completes in < 150ms and occupies < 20MB in memory (actually runs around ~1.26ms and takes ~500kb). No O(N^2) loops detected in IPC serialization (standard Serde JSON serialization applied on vector).
